  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nA ridge of high pressure centered over the Pacific coast continues \nto break down tonight and Friday, with increasing westerly winds \nahead of an approaching cold front leading to another day of warm, \ndry, and windy conditions over the region for Friday. Wind gusts \nduring the day are forecast to be in the 30-40 mph range, although \nthe usual locations nearby mountain ranges could see gusts of 50+ \nmph. A Red Flag Warning has been issued for areas with the most \nfavorable fuel conditions; see the below FIRE WEATHER section for \ndetails.\n\nThis cold front is associated with a Gulf of Alaska upper level low \nejecting south from otherwise strong zonal flow over Canada. This \ninitial push of cooler air Friday evening into the overnight brings \nlight precipitation mainly to the Rocky Mountain Front and \nimmediately adjacent areas with snow expected above about\n6000 feet.\n\nAs the upper low pinches south late Friday and Saturday, it \nelongates in a positive orientation, extending from the California \ncoast into Central Canada...leaving Montana squarely under broad \nsouthwest flow and in a broad baroclinic zone. Models continue to \nfavor the dominant vorticity maxima associated with the upper \nlevel trough splitting from the northern stream, allowing high \npressure to build into Alberta...providing a reliable feed of \ncooler, drier air from the north...and also providing enhanced \nconvergence and developing FGEN over central and west-central MT. \nThis combination of crashing temperatures and increasing coverage \nof precipitation is expected to bring snow levels to valley floors\nSaturday evening with locally intense snowfall from mesoscale \nsnow banding Saturday night through Sunday morning. The northerly \nflow regime north of the I-90 corridor also favors upsloping into \nthe southern RMF and Little Belt Mountains, adding to confidence \nin impacts in this part of the forecast area.\n\nFor snowfall totals...the most likely scenario predicts 6-12 inches \nfor mountainous areas, although the most favored upslope areas and \nareas under a mesoscale band have potential for 18+ inches through \nSunday. The lower elevations are significantly trickier; warm \nsurface temperatures upon onset will limit accumulation to some \ndegree despite crashing temperatures, however by Sunday conditions \nare expected to be cool enough for mostly all snow to fall at all\nelevations.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nAs is typically the case with shoulder-season snowfall events, the\nwet nature of the snow is a concern. Many trees still have leaves,\nand as wet snow accumulates and temperatures continue to decrease\nthere is concern for damage to trees and power lines especially \nas wind gusts increase during the Saturday night/early Sunday \ntimeframe. There remains considerable uncertainty regarding the \nrain-snow transition, especially along and south of the I-90 \ncorridor as the influence of tropical remnants threatens to enter \nthe picture. In any case, this upcoming holiday weekend will be \nmuch cooler and damp, even if not seeing significant snowfall. \nOutdoor activities including hunting, camping, etc will be \nsignificantly impacted by these conditions.",
